A straightforward version is to try every substring and check it from both ends. I only update the answer when the current substring is longer, so many short checks are skipped after a good answer is found.

For example, with `s = "babad"`, the single letters are checked first, then `"bab"` becomes the best match. The method also returns an empty string when the input is empty.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    bool isPalindrome(const string& s, int left, int right) {
        while (left < right) {
            if (s[left] != s[right]) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

public:
    string longestPalindrome(string s) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return "";
        }

        int bestStart = 0;
        int bestLength = 1;

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            for (int end = start + bestLength; end < n; end++) {
                int length = end - start + 1;
                if (isPalindrome(s, start, end)) {
                    if (length > bestLength) {
                        bestStart = start;
                        bestLength = length;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return s.substr(bestStart, bestLength);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)` in the worst case, because there are `O(n^2)` ranges and each check can take `O(n)` time.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned string.

Instead of filling the table from the outside, this version asks recursively whether a range is a palindrome and saves the result. If the same range is requested again, the saved value is returned immediately.

The recursion stops when the pointers meet or cross, because a range with zero or one character is always a palindrome. It also handles an empty input before creating the table.

```java
class Solution {
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) {
            return "";
        }

        int n = s.length();
        int[][] memo = new int[n][n];
        int bestStart = 0;
        int bestLength = 1;

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            for (int end = start; end < n; end++) {
                if (isPalindrome(s, start, end, memo)) {
                    int length = end - start + 1;
                    if (length > bestLength) {
                        bestStart = start;
                        bestLength = length;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return s.substring(bestStart, bestStart + bestLength);
    }

    private boolean isPalindrome(String s, int left, int right, int[][] memo) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return true;
        }

        if (memo[left][right] != 0) {
            return memo[left][right] == 1;
        }

        if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
            memo[left][right] = -1;
            return false;
        }

        boolean insideIsPalindrome = isPalindrome(s, left + 1, right - 1, memo);
        memo[left][right] = insideIsPalindrome ? 1 : -1;
        return insideIsPalindrome;
    }
}
```

A dry run on `"racecar"` eventually checks the outside `r` characters after the inner `"aceca"` result is saved as true, so the full string becomes the answer.

The time complexity is `O(n^2)`, since each range is computed at most once. The memo table uses `O(n^2)` space, and the recursive call stack can use `O(n)` space.

This version uses Manacher's algorithm. I add separator characters so odd and even palindromes can be handled in the same loop. `radius[i]` stores how far the palindrome reaches around position `i` in the new string.

The useful part is reusing the radius from the mirrored position. It avoids checking characters that are already known to match. This is faster than the usual quadratic methods, though the indexing is a little harder to debug.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string longestPalindrome(string s) {
        if (s.empty()) {
            return "";
        }

        string transformed = "^";
        for (char ch : s) {
            transformed += '#';
            transformed += ch;
        }
        transformed += "#$";

        int n = static_cast<int>(transformed.size());
        vector<int> radius(n, 0);
        int center = 0;
        int right = 0;
        int bestCenter = 0;
        int bestRadius = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int mirror = 2 * center - i;

            if (i < right) {
                radius[i] = min(right - i, radius[mirror]);
            }

            while (transformed[i + 1 + radius[i]]
                   == transformed[i - 1 - radius[i]]) {
                radius[i]++;
            }

            if (i + radius[i] > right) {
                center = i;
                right = i + radius[i];
            }

            if (radius[i] > bestRadius) {
                bestRadius = radius[i];
                bestCenter = i;
            }
        }

        int start = (bestCenter - bestRadius) / 2;
        return s.substr(start, bestRadius);
    }
};
```

For `"aaaa"`, the radius grows through the whole transformed string and returns the complete input. The algorithm takes `O(n)` time and uses `O(n)` extra space.

I used a table where `dp[start][end]` says whether the characters from `start` through `end` form a palindrome. A longer range is valid when its two outside characters match and the inside range is already valid.

The table is filled by increasing the ending index. That makes the smaller inside range available before it is needed. For `"abba"`, the table first marks `"bb"`, then uses it to mark the full string.

```python
class Solution:
    def longestPalindrome(self, s: str) -> str:
        n = len(s)
        if n == 0:
            return ""

        dp = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]
        best_start = 0
        best_length = 1

        for end in range(n):
            dp[end][end] = True

            for start in range(end - 1, -1, -1):
                if s[start] != s[end]:
                    continue

                length = end - start + 1
                if length == 2 or dp[start + 1][end - 1]:
                    dp[start][end] = True
                    if length > best_length:
                        best_start = start
                        best_length = length

        return s[best_start:best_start + best_length]
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because each start and end pair is considered once.

Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the Boolean table. This uses more memory than center expansion, but the palindrome condition is easy to follow.

Every palindrome has either one center character or a gap between two center characters. I expand from both kinds of centers and keep the longest range.

A small dry run with `"cbbd"`: the odd centers only find one-character strings, but the even center between the two `b` characters finds `"bb"`. Checking both center types is important.

```java
class Solution {
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) {
            return "";
        }

        int bestStart = 0;
        int bestEnd = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            int oddLength = expand(s, i, i);
            int evenLength = expand(s, i, i + 1);
            int length = Math.max(oddLength, evenLength);

            if (length > bestEnd - bestStart + 1) {
                bestStart = i - (length - 1) / 2;
                bestEnd = i + length / 2;
            }
        }

        return s.substring(bestStart, bestEnd + 1);
    }

    private int expand(String s, int left, int right) {
        while (left >= 0 && right < s.length()
                && s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)) {
            left--;
            right++;
        }

        return right - left - 1;
    }
}
```

There are `2n - 1` possible centers. Each one can expand across the string, so the time complexity is `O(n^2)`. The extra space is `O(1)`.

The empty string and a one-character string are handled before the loop. A one-character input is already a valid answer.

# Intuition

A palindrome reads the same from left to right and right to left.

Example:

```text
aba
racecar
abba
```

The important observation is:

```text
Every palindrome expands from its center.
```

For odd length palindrome:

```text
aba
 ^
```

For even length palindrome:

```text
abba
 ^^
```

So for every index, we try:

* expanding with one center `(i,i)` → odd palindrome
* expanding with two centers `(i,i+1)` → even palindrome

While characters on both sides are equal, we keep expanding outward and track the longest palindrome found.

---

# Approach

We create a helper function `expandfromcenter()`.

This function:

* takes left and right pointers
* expands outward while characters are equal
* returns palindrome length

For every index:

1. Find longest odd palindrome
2. Find longest even palindrome
3. Take maximum length
4. Update answer if larger palindrome found

Finally return the substring using stored start and end indexes.

---

# Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:

    int expandfromcenter(const string &s, int left, int right){

        while(left >= 0 && right < s.size() && s[left] == s[right]){
            left--;
            right++;
        }

        return right - left - 1;
    }

    string longestPalindrome(string s) {

        int start = 0;
        int end = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < s.size(); i++){

            int odd = expandfromcenter(s, i, i);

            int even = expandfromcenter(s, i, i + 1);

            int maxlen = max(odd, even);

            if(maxlen > end - start + 1){

                start = i - (maxlen - 1) / 2;

                end = i + maxlen / 2;
            }
        }

        return s.substr(start, end - start + 1);
    }
};
```

